findings.recommendation:
Replace `for try await line in bytes.lines` with a manual byte-level iterator that accumulates into a `Data` buffer and aborts as soon as the running size exceeds 65_536 bytes (still without a newline). Sketch:
```swift
var lineBuf = Data()
for try await byte in bytes {
    if byte == 0x0A {                              // LF
        let line = String(data: lineBuf, encoding: .utf8) ?? ""
        lineBuf.removeAll(keepingCapacity: true)
        // process `line` exactly as today
    } else {
        if lineBuf.count >= Self.maxSSELineBytes {
            throw AIProviderError.streamingError("SSE line exceeds 64 KB cap")
        }
        lineBuf.append(byte)
    }
}
```
Alternative: keep `bytes.lines` but document in the file header that the cap is best-effort and that the true upper bound is whatever `URLSession.AsyncBytes` will tolerate — and update Decision 10 §7 to match. Recommended path is the manual iterator: it makes the cap real and also gives you control for finding (2) below.

Wrap the entire body of `streamCompletion` (or at minimum the two pre-loop `try await` calls) in a do/catch that re-throws via `sanitize`. Cheapest implementation:
```swift
func streamCompletion(...) async throws {
    do {
        // existing body — including the request build and bytes(for:) call
    } catch let error as AIProviderError {
        throw error
    } catch {
        throw AIProviderError.streamingError(sanitize(String(describing: error)))
    }
}
```
This closes the loop and makes the invariant self-evident from the function shape. The existing inner do/catch at line 254 then becomes redundant and can be removed (or kept for clarity).
